{
 "about": {
  "what": "Step 2d-i: the share of the paper's grain (its fine pattern of lighter and darker pixels, the photo minus a smoothed copy, in A) that shows in each post-image.  white_study.carryover().",
  "A": "absorbance, -log10 of a reflectance ratio; bigger means darker.  0.001 A is about 0.23 % of the light.",
  "match": "the mean, over the pad's interior, of one photo's grain times another's, pixel by pixel, after lining them up, taken at the extra shift (up to 1 px) that makes it largest.  Units A^2.  Large when the two show the same pattern; near 0 when unrelated.",
  "holder, sample, file": "cassette holder, sample number, repeat file name",
  "rep": "repeat number in the series, 0 = first (capture order, confirmed from the photo time stamps)",
  "cell": "channel: LED name . Bayer layer",
  "D": "pre with pre: mean match between different pre-images of the strip.  The full strength of the paper's grain.  A^2.",
  "N": "post with pre: mean match of the pre-images with this post-image.  A^2.",
  "c": "N / D, no units.  1 = the post-image shows the whole grain.",
  "E": "post with post: mean match between different post-images of the strip (paper grain as it shows after the test, plus the dye's own fine pattern).  A^2; sqrt(E) is in A.",
  "dye_A": "how dark the dye is in this channel: interior median of -log10(R_post / R_pre), the file's own pre-image lined up onto the post-image.  A.",
  "shift": "mean |row shift|, |column shift| of the best matches, layer pixels (one layer pixel = 2 sensor pixels)."
 },
